      },
      "urpx:hasCoverageClaim": {
        "@id": "jemc:cc-gstou-2026-01-01",
        "@type": "urpx:CoverageClaim",
        "urpx:completenessResult": {
          "@id": "urpx:partial"
        },
        "urpx:selfContained": true,
        "urpx:explainedBy": {
          "@type": "urpx:Evaluation",
          "@id": "jemc:cc-gstou-2026-01-01-evaluation",
          "urpx:evaluationKind": {
            "@id": "urpx:coverageEvaluation"
          },
          "urpx:description": "This filing does not carry everything its source document states. 5 parts are deferred, listed here in the same words as the filing's tariff.toml [modeling].deferrals so the two records cannot drift. (1) Billing Demand ratchets. Each billing demand is a calculated MetricInput, the Non-Coincident Billing Demand from the highest 60-minute kW of the current month and the preceding eleven, at least 90 percent of the highest summer demand and 65 percent of the highest winter demand, and the On-Peak Billing Demand from the highest 60-minute kW in On-Peak hours with a 90 percent ratchet on the previous summer months; its formula is text in its CalculationMethod, which URPX v0.5.1 does not evaluate, so a consumer has to compute it from twelve months of demand history. The sheet's seasons for it are billing months, June through September and October through May. (2) Wholesale Power Cost Adjustment factor. Schedule P-22 increases or decreases every bill by a per-kWh factor the Cooperative computes each month from its estimated purchased-power costs and sales, and publishes no factor. The JSON-LD defines the adjustment as a per-kWh PriceDefinition with no Price, so a modeled bill is high or low by the month's factor times its kWh. (3) Excess Reactive Demand. Where a power factor below 95 percent lagging is indicated, the Cooperative may install metering for reactive demand and bills kVAR above one-third of the measured kW at $0.30 per kVAR. The JSON-LD carries the $0.30 on a calculated MetricInput whose formula is text in its CalculationMethod; a consumer has to compute the excess kVAR, and supplies none for a customer the Cooperative does not meter for it. (4) Minimum Monthly Charge. The minimum monthly charge is the greater of two options: (1) the greater of $12.00 per kW of (a) the highest on-peak demand measured during the billing months June through September or (b) 65 percent of the highest demand measured during the billing months October through May or the summer off-peak demand, never less than the Service Charge plus excess kVAR charges; or (2) a charge specified in a supplemental contract. A minimum is a floor under the bill, which no Price expresses, so it is not carried; a modeled bill below it is low by the difference. (5) Terms of Payment and Contract Period. The sheet adds a late fee and disconnection charges under the Service Rules and Regulations, makes all charges subject to applicable taxes, and sets a one-year contract. None is priced on the sheet and none is carried.",
          "urpx:evaluatedOn": "2026-10-18T00:00:00"
        }
      }
    }
  }
